{
  "rationale": "The only race evidence is the baseline in iteration 0, and it is a clear failure. All 60 trials went off track on all three development tracks, with none finishing. Progress was only 0.11 to 0.16, max_error sat at about 0.06 (the off-track limit), and each run logged 1 to 2 line-loss events. The off-track limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4). Body width can't raise it above the 0.06 floor within the allowed 0.08 to 0.18 range, so body width is not a lever. The line is lost once the tape's edge leaves all sensors, which for the current +/-0.03 span happens at about 0.03 + 0.0125 + 0.006 = 0.048 m of lateral offset. That is close to the 0.06 m off-track limit, so the robot loses the line and then leaves the track before it can recover. The fix is to widen the sensor span so the outer sensors keep the tape in view during the error excursions that precede the loss. I will use seven sensors at 0.02 m spacing across x = -0.06 to +0.06, which stays inside the +/-0.08 limit and the 7-sensor maximum. Spacing of 20 mm is still finer than the 25 mm tape, so the centre of the tape is always resolved by at least one or two sensors. Sensor size (0.012), sensor y (0.05), wheel track, wheel radius, body and mass are held fixed so that the race isolates the effect of the span change. Expected result: fewer line-loss events, lower max_error, and at least some runs that finish on the oval and s-bend. The slalom may still leave the track because it needs faster response. If the next race still shows line loss, the next step is to widen further or adjust sensor y. If the race shows oscillation instead, the next step is to reduce the sensor lead by moving y toward 0.03.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.06, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.04, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.04, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.06, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.16,
    "mass": 0.8
  },
  "intent": "Widen the sensor span from +/-0.03 (five sensors) to +/-0.06 (seven sensors at 0.02 m spacing), keeping every other geometry field from iteration 0. Baseline evidence showed line loss at about 0.048 m offset, just before the 0.06 m off-track limit, with all 60 trials going off track. Wider outer sensors should keep the tape visible for longer during excursions, reducing line-loss events and max_error and letting some runs finish on the oval and s-bend.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort"]
  },
  "learning": "Hypotheses: (1) With the span at +/-0.06, the line is lost only at offsets above about 0.07 m, which is beyond the off-track limit, so line_loss_events should drop to about zero on the oval and s-bend. (2) Seven sensors at 0.02 m spacing with 0.012 m footprints still give a near-linear centre-line signal within about +/-0.03 m. (3) The slalom will still be the hardest track, because its sharper alternation needs faster steering response than the geometry alone provides. Next measurements to request: per-track line_loss_events and max_error to confirm whether the span fix worked, and steering_oscillation to check whether the wider span adds jitter from more sensors seeing the tape at once.",
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Widen sensor span to 0.06",
      "notes": "Seven sensors at 0.02 m spacing across plus or minus 0.06 m, replacing five at plus or minus 0.03. Baseline evidence: all 60 trials went off track with 1 to 2 line-loss events, and line loss begins near 0.048 m offset, so the outer sensors need to see the tape for longer."
    }
  }
}
